<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romania</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romania is a country in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, formed in the nineteenth century from the union of the principalities of Wallachia and Moldavia. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it was greatly enlarged with territories, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a>, taken from the dissolved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>. Its lands were earlier part of the cultural and religious sphere of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, as accounts of the Byzantine Empire note.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romania.jpg" alt="Romania" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AdiJapan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Romania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's break-up in 1918 created or enlarged seven states, Austria, Hungary, Czechoslovakia, Yugoslavia, Poland, <b>Romania</b> and Italy, along lines that satisfied the principle of national self-determination only imperfectly, leaving large German-speaking minorities in Czechoslovakia, Hungarian minorities in Romania, Czechoslovakia and Yugoslavia, and other grievances that fuelled the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Orthodox Christianity that the Byzantine state patronised and defined became the faith of Russia, Serbia, Bulgaria, <b>Romania</b>, Georgia and the Greek world — a “Byzantine commonwealth” that extended the empire's cultural reach far beyond its political frontiers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its Sigmaringen branch, created in the division of 1576, produced Charles Anthony, minister-president of Prussia from 1858 to 1862, and his son Charles, who became Prince of <b>Romania</b> in 1866 and King Carol I in 1881, founding the Romanian royal house.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hohenzollern">County of Hohenzollern · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Republic relied on alliances: the Little Entente with Yugoslavia and <b>Romania</b>, a treaty with France in 1924 and a pact with the Soviet Union in 1935 that obliged Moscow to help only if France acted first.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Basic treaties with <b>Romania</b>, Slovakia and Ukraine renounced territorial claims, though the rights of the roughly five million Hungarians living abroad, mostly in Romania, Slovakia and Serbia, still cause tensions; relations with Serbia grew close, while those with Ukraine deteriorated from 2017 over the Hungarian minority there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hungary">Hungary · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Kingdom of Greece was the first new nation state carved out of the Ottoman Empire, and its creation by the intervention and guarantee of the great powers set a pattern followed by Serbia, <b>Romania</b> and Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
